## For Claude Code: instructions

You are setting up multiple isolated instances of the Claude desktop app, one per account. Read this whole guide before acting. Facts below were verified 2026-07-11 on macOS with Claude desktop v1.20186.1; re-verify anything version-sensitive on the target machine rather than assuming.

### Ground rules (non-negotiable)

1. **Never modify, move, rename, or duplicate `/Applications/Claude.app`.** Duplicated copies fall out of sync when the app auto-updates. Every launcher points at the one real app.
2. **Never write into the user's existing default profile** (`~/Library/Application Support/Claude`). It stays the primary account's home, untouched.
3. **Never launch the same profile twice.** On a Mac, the app has NO single-instance lock: two processes started with the same `--user-data-dir` will happily share one data folder, which risks corrupting it. The launchers you build below guard against this; also don't do it yourself during testing.
4. **Sign-ins are the user's job.** Never handle their passwords. Sign-ins must happen with only ONE instance running: the `claude://` OAuth callback is delivered to whichever instance is open, so with two open it can land in the wrong one.
5. **Mind your own runtime.** If you are running inside the Claude desktop app, quitting that app kills your session, so hand any step requiring a quit to the user as written instructions. Sandboxed sessions often cannot read or write `~/Library`; if you hit that, tell the user to run this guide from Claude Code in a terminal instead.
6. **Nothing here is destructive.** The only thing you may delete is a scratch/test profile you created yourself in this session.

### Step 1: Gather inputs

Ask the user (if they didn't already say): the two account emails, and a short label for each (defaults: "Personal" and "Work"). Conventions used below:

- The **primary** account is whichever is signed into the app right now. It keeps the default profile and gets launcher "Claude \<Label1\>".
- The **second** account gets a new profile folder `~/Library/Application Support/Claude-<Label2>` and launcher "Claude \<Label2\>".
- More than two accounts work the same way: one profile folder and one launcher per extra account. Make sure no profile folder name is a prefix of another (e.g. avoid `Claude-Work` plus `Claude-Work2`), because the launcher guards match on that name.

### Step 2: Preflight checks

- Confirm the app exists and note its version: `defaults read "/Applications/Claude.app/Contents/Info.plist" CFBundleShortVersionString`
- List existing profiles: `ls ~/Library/Application\ Support/ | grep -i claude` (expect `Claude`; leave anything else alone)
- See what's running: `ps axo pid,command | grep -e '/Applications/Claude\.app/Contents/MacOS/[C]laude' | grep -v -e '--type='` (main processes only; the `[C]` trick stops grep matching itself)
- Confirm `/Applications` is writable; fall back to `~/Applications` for the launchers if not.

### Step 3: Verify the mechanism with a throwaway profile

Before building anything, prove this app version honors the flag. Warn the user a test window will briefly appear, then:

```sh
open -n -a "/Applications/Claude.app" --args --user-data-dir="/tmp/claude-profile-test"
```

Within a few seconds you should see (a) a new main process whose command line carries that flag, and (b) `/tmp/claude-profile-test` populated with Cookies, Local Storage, Preferences, etc. That confirms isolation works. Then clean up: kill only PIDs whose command line contains the scratch path, and `rm -rf` the scratch folder.

If no isolated profile appears, STOP: this app version no longer honors `--user-data-dir`, and the approach needs rethinking. Tell the user.

### Step 4: Build the guarded launchers

Each launcher is a small AppleScript app. Logic: find this account's running instance via `ps`; if found, focus it by process id (never relaunch, per ground rule 3); if not found, launch a new instance with the right profile. Compile each with `osacompile -o "/Applications/Claude <Label>.app" <source.applescript>`.

**Launcher for the primary account** (default profile; note it detects a main process with NO `user-data-dir` in its command line, which is what a plain launch looks like):

```applescript
set pidText to do shell script "ps axo pid=,command= | grep -e '/Applications/Claude\\.app/Contents/MacOS/[C]laude' | grep -v -e '--type=' | grep -v -e 'user-data-dir' | awk '{print $1}' | head -n1"
if pidText is "" then
	do shell script "open -n -a '/Applications/Claude.app'"
else
	try
		tell application "System Events" to set frontmost of (first process whose unix id is (pidText as integer)) to true
	end try
end if
```

**Launcher for the second account** (replace `Claude-Work` with the actual profile folder name, in both places):

```applescript
set pidText to do shell script "ps axo pid=,command= | grep -e '/Applications/Claude\\.app/Contents/MacOS/[C]laude' | grep -v -e '--type=' | grep -F 'Claude-Work' | awk '{print $1}' | head -n1"
if pidText is "" then
	do shell script "open -n -a '/Applications/Claude.app' --args --user-data-dir=\"$HOME/Library/Application Support/Claude-Work\""
else
	try
		tell application "System Events" to set frontmost of (first process whose unix id is (pidText as integer)) to true
	end try
end if
```

Notes for you, the agent:

- The `[C]laude` character-class trick and the `try` block are load-bearing. The first prevents the pipeline from matching its own shell process (which would make the launcher think an instance is running when none is). The second makes a denied Automation permission harmless.
- The first time a launcher takes the focus path, macOS asks the user to let it control "System Events". Tell the user to click Allow (denying only disables auto-focus).
- Smoke-test the second-account launcher: open it, verify exactly one new main process with the new profile folder, then kill that instance and `rm -rf` the new profile folder so the user's first real sign-in starts pristine. Do NOT smoke-test the double-click/focus path (it would trigger the permission prompt at a moment the user isn't expecting).

### Step 5 (optional): custom launcher icons

The default script-scroll applet icon already distinguishes launchers from the real app. If the user wants custom icons: take their square image, render it centered at 824px on a transparent 1024px canvas (Apple's icon grid; a full-bleed image looks oversized in the Dock), generate an iconset (`sips -z` at 16/32/128/256/512 plus @2x each), build with `iconutil -c icns`, overwrite `Contents/Resources/applet.icns` inside the launcher, re-sign with `codesign --force --sign -`, then `killall Dock` (warn the user the Dock will blink). Never re-icon the real app.

### Step 6: Hand off the sign-in (the user does this)

Give the user these steps, then wait:

1. Drag both launchers from Applications into the Dock.
2. Quit Claude fully (click its window, Cmd+Q). If your Claude Code session lives inside that app, it pauses here; everything needed is already written down.
3. Click "Claude \<Label2\>" in the Dock. A fresh instance opens; sign in with the second account.
4. Click "Claude \<Label1\>" (or the plain Claude icon). The primary account is still signed in.
5. From now on, switch accounts by clicking the launchers. Both instances can run at once.

Dock cosmetics to warn them about: while both run, the two RUNNING tiles show the identical Claude icon (unavoidable without duplicating the app, which is forbidden), and after a Dock restart macOS sometimes coalesces them into one tile. Both states are harmless; the launchers always find the right instance because they go by process, not by Dock tile. To quit one instance only: click its window first, then Cmd+Q.

### Step 7: Migrate per-account things (check, don't assume)

- **claude.ai-side things follow the login automatically:** conversations, projects, account-level connectors. Nothing to do.
- **Desktop-app-side MCP servers do not.** `claude_desktop_config.json` lives inside each profile folder, so the new instance starts with none. Copy over any entries the second account needs from the old profile's config, then restart that instance.
- **Scheduled tasks are per account and only run while that account is signed in inside a running instance.** Tasks made in the app's chat live at `<profile>/local-agent-mode-sessions/<org-uuid>/<account-uuid>/scheduled-tasks.json`, and Claude Code's at `<profile>/claude-code-sessions/<org-uuid>/<account-uuid>/scheduled-tasks.json`. If the user previously juggled both accounts in one app, the old default profile may contain a registry under the SECOND account's context; those tasks will never run again once the default app stays signed into the primary account. Migrate them:
  1. After the user's first sign-in, the new profile creates its own `<org-uuid>/<account-uuid>/` folders. Verify they match the old profile's second-account context (the UUIDs are account-derived and stable).
  2. With the second instance QUIT (it reads the registry at launch), write the task entries into the new profile's matching `scheduled-tasks.json`. Copy fields exactly; drop stale `notifySessionId` values (they point at sessions in the old profile) and keep `lastRunAt` so the scheduler doesn't fire catch-up runs.
  3. Set `"enabled": false` on the old copies, in place. Keep that file as a backup; do not delete it.
  4. Have the user relaunch and ask that instance to list its scheduled tasks with next run times. Next runs a few minutes past the cron time are normal (the app staggers runs).
- **`~/.claude` is shared by all instances** (Claude Code config, skills, global CLAUDE.md, session history), since it's per Mac user, not per profile. Nothing to migrate, but tell the user: anything in their global CLAUDE.md now applies to sessions in every instance, so account-specific instructions belong in per-folder CLAUDE.md files instead.

### Step 8: Verify, then document

Verify: both instances running at once with distinct profiles (`ps` check from step 2); each launcher focuses rather than relaunches its running instance; migrated scheduled tasks (if any) listed with sane next-run times.

Finally, write the user a README.md (in a folder they choose) recording: what was created and where, the per-profile paths, the rules (sign in with one instance open, never double-launch a profile, never duplicate the app), what's shared vs. separate, and the recovery procedure below. Future sessions, and future humans, will thank you.

**Recovery, for the README:** to reset the second account's instance, quit it, delete its profile folder, relaunch its launcher, sign in fresh. The primary profile is never involved. To undo the entire setup, delete the launchers and the extra profile folder(s).

