## Facts reference (verified 2026-07-11, macOS, Claude desktop v1.20186.1)

- `--user-data-dir` is honored and gives full profile isolation (login, cookies, settings, connectors, conversations cache).
- The app holds NO single-instance lock: same-profile double launches really do produce two processes on one folder. Hence the guarded launchers. This is a macOS fact; on Windows, version 2.42304 reportedly added a lock (see the appendix).
- `claude://` login callbacks route to whichever instance is running: sign in with exactly one open.
- One shared app bundle means one auto-update path; after an update it's good hygiene to relaunch both instances.
- Scheduled-task registries are per account under `local-agent-mode-sessions/<org-uuid>/<account-uuid>/`, and the org/account UUIDs are stable across profiles for the same account.
- Sandboxed sessions may be blocked from `~/Library` and similar host paths; profile-folder surgery needs a full-access Claude Code session or manual hands.
- Dock tiles for two instances of one app can coalesce into a single tile after the Dock process restarts; cosmetic only.